    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a 2D coordinate system, you can use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\ndistance = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the coordinates are (-3, -4) for (x1, y1) and (4, -5) for (x2, y2).\n\nLet's plug in the values and calculate the distance:\n\n\\[\ndistance = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nWe can break down the calculation step by step:\n\n1. Calculate the differences in the x and y coordinates:\n   \\[\n   x_2 - x_1 = 4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y_2 - y_1 = -5 - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1\n   \\]\n\n2. Square these differences:\n   \\[\n   (x_2 - x_1)^2 = 7^2 = 49\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (y_2 - y_1)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the squared differences and take the square root:\n   \\[\n   distance = \\sqrt{49 + 1} = \\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the distance between (-3, -4) and (4, -5) is \\(5\\sqrt{2}\\) units.",
